@ 10.2.6 - Proto-Malayo-Polynesian to Proto-North Sarawak
# citation: Whimemsz, from Blust, Robert (2002), “Kiput Historical Phonology”. Oceanic Linguistics 41(2):384 – 438; and Blust, Robert (2007), “Òma Lóngh Historical Phonology”. Oceanic Linguistics 46(1):1 – 53 Nasal assimilation to following stops in some words; in other words it results in a geminate stop Postvocalic obstruents with different POAs become geminates of the second when “in reduplicated monosyllabic roots” and “in non-reduplicated bases which had undergone the change of schwa syncope in medial syllables”
	q > ʔ
	{ʔ,h} > ∅ / #_
	{ʔ,h} > ∅ / V:[+high]_V
	h > ∅ / _#
	h > ʔ / V_V
	h > ʔ / :{ a_i, a_u }:
	ə > ∅ / adjacent to a vowel
	a > ə / _UU{ …#, # }
	ə > ∅ / _V
	ə > ∅ VC_CV
	C > C:[+long] / ə_V (?)
	{ ɟ, ɟ:[+long] } > { d, d:[+long] }
	b:[+long] d:[+long] d͡z:[+long] ɡ:[+long] > bʱ dʱ d͡zʱ ɡʱ (Whimemsz says these become “voiced stops with voiceless releases. . .treated as unit phonemes, not clusters)”
	ə > ∅ / #_UU{ …#, # } (i.e., in word-initial position in prepenultimate syllables)
	{ p:[+long], t:[+long], c:[+long], k:[+long] } > { p, t, c, k }
	c > s